amcedvd.library: DVD-Video navigation for AmigaMCE
==================================================

What it is
----------
libdvdread 7.1.1 and libdvdnav 7.0.0 (VideoLAN, extras/libdvd) built as
an Exec library, behind an interface of AmigaMCE's own. The opener reads
the disc (src/dvddisc.c: a drive through HD_SCSICMD, or an image file);
the library reads the disc's file system and IFOs, runs the DVD's
virtual machine (menus, buttons, titles, chapters, angles, still frames)
and hands back the MPEG-2 Program Stream one 2048-byte block at a time,
with events for what the player must do (flush, still, palette, streams,
highlight). Decoding is the player's: the ZZ9000's media session.

Files
-----
  include/libraries/amcedvd.h  the interface: structures, events, constants
  amcedvd_lib.sfd              the calls and their registers
  include/clib, inline, proto  the headers for callers, from the .sfd
  amcedvd.c                    the library: the calls, on libdvdnav
  port.c, port.h, amitime.c    the C library libdvd calls, inside a library
  port/                        headers in place of the system's for libdvd
                               (amiport.h is forced into every file; config.h
                               and the version headers stand in for meson's)

  ../../extras/libdvd          libdvdread and libdvdnav's sources, unchanged
                               but for the three lines in its CHANGES file
  ../../tests/dvd_test.c       the tests (make test-dvd-host, make test-dvd)
  ../../tests/mkdvd.sh         the test disc (tests/fixtures/dvd/test.iso.xz)

Built with make dvdlib (build/dvdlib/amcedvd.library).

Licences
--------
The library as a whole is GPL-2.0-or-later, as libdvdread and libdvdnav
are (their COPYING files in extras/libdvd). amcedvd.c, port.c, port.h,
amitime.c and port/ are (c) 2026 Brian King, under the same licence.

The interface is not: include/libraries/amcedvd.h, amcedvd_lib.sfd and
the clib, inline and proto headers are AmigaMCE's own declarations,
written for it, (c) 2026 Brian King, under AmigaMCE's MIT licence. They
contain nothing of libdvdread's or libdvdnav's. AmigaMCE (MIT) compiles
only these, and opens the library at run time, as it does amcesql.library
and mpega.library; no GPL code is compiled into AmigaMCE.

Whoever is given the library must be able to get its complete source:
when it ships (in the release archive, Extras, or anywhere else), this
folder, extras/libdvd and the Makefile's dvdlib part go with it.

Inside an Exec library
----------------------
- No C startup runs, so libdvd's malloc, printf and the rest are port.c's:
  memory from exec (AllocVec), messages into the opener's log callback,
  no files (libdvd opens paths only when given one; the library only
  uses its stream form).
- One task uses a handle at a time (several handles may be open). The
  library's data is shared by its openers (libnix's libinit).
- libdvd runs on the caller's stack, and opening a title needs about 30 KB of
  it (libdvdread's UDF lookup keeps sector buffers there: 14.5 KB in
  UDFFindFile alone): give the calling process 48 KB.
- An abort() inside libdvd (a NULL where one cannot be, a domain libdvdnav
  cannot be in) ends that call with DVDE_ERROR or FALSE; the handle is
  then only closed. A failed assertion is logged and libdvd goes on, as
  it does built without assertions.
- No floating point (two random-number lines changed to integers) and
  64-bit seeks (one line), so off_t's 32 bits on AmigaOS do not matter.
- Not yet: CSS-encrypted discs (dvdcss.library, later), images of 2 GB or
  more (src/dvddisc.c), and the read-ahead cache is off unless
  DVDF_READAHEAD is given (it takes up to 1 MB at a time).
- Known: libdvdnav 7.0.0 reads past a program chain's cells on some
  deliberately corrupted IFOs (found by fuzzing the test disc); on an
  Amiga that reads stray memory rather than stopping. Real discs with
  read errors fail to open or end with DVDE_ERROR instead.
